\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The wind pressed itself against the cliffside with a restless howl, combing through the tall grasses until they bent and swayed in unison, thousands of green blades moving like the surface of the sea. The land stretched wide and lonely here, untamed and solemn, as though the world had forgotten.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 At the farthest edge of that cliff stood a tower. A lighthouse, it seemed at first glance\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 its stone walls weathered by salt and moisture, its frame stubborn against the bite of the wind. Yet no lantern had ever burned at its crown. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 This particular lighthouse has been transformed into an apothecary, a strange refuge whose quiet purpose was known only to those who sought it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Upon its rooftop sat a boy, no more than twenty, with his brunette hair left unruly by the wind. He leaned against the cold terracotta roof tiles, his gaze fixed far beyond The Great Lake of Glyndoria, far beyond its beacons into the grand lands of Solivia just beyond it. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Between his fingers a cigarette smoldered, its faint crackle nearly lost to the roar of the gale. He drew in the smoke slowly, filling himself with it, and exhaled into the open air. The gray tendrils dissolved at once, caught and scattered by the currents, vanishing into the vastness\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 like his thoughts, like his worries, becoming weightless for a breath.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Edward!\u8221\'94 A voice called from within the tower, muffled by walls, distant yet insistent.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, shit,\u8221\'94 he muttered under his breath. With hurried fingers he flicked the cigarette over the cliff\u8217\'92s edge and watched the ember vanish into the swaying grass below. He brushed the smoke away.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Coming, Mom!\u8221\'94 he shouted back, his words carried off at once by the wind. For a moment longer he lingered, eyes on the horizon, as if reluctant to let go of whatever it was he had almost grasped. Then, with a sigh, he rose and disappeared into the tower.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Eddie steadied himself on the sloping tiles, each step measured, the terracotta rough and moist from the crashing tides. The wind clawed at his white shirt and tugged at his balance, but he moved with agile familliarity, careful not to let his boots slip on the smooth, curved clay.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Ahead, his window gaped open, the curtains billowing outward like pale banners. Beyond them, the lighthouse walls groaned under the weight of the wind, and from far below came the endless crash of waves breaking against stone. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He crept closer, finding his footing between the rows of tiles until at last his hand caught the sill. With a final glance at the horizon, he swung his weight inward and slipped through the window.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The moment he drew the frame shut, the world seemed to collapse inward. The roar of the gale cut off, the pounding surf became a muffled murmur, and the curtains fell limp against the glass. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Silence. Until even his own breath felt too loud. He allowed himself a small, guilty smile, savoring the satisfaction of shutting out the storm.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 But he was not alone.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 In the half-light of the room, Mrs. Welton stood waiting. Her arms were folded across her chest, her figure rigid, her eyes sharp with the kind of disapproval that needed no words. She had the look of someone who had long grown tired of cigarettes, windswept rooftops, and the reckless habits of her son. Eddie froze, the silence between them louder than any storm, and for the first time all evening, he wished the window had stayed open}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93How many times have I told you not to climb up onto that rooftop! Those tiles have been there for centuries! centuries, Edward! Do you have any idea what could\u8217\'92ve happened if you slipped? And\u8230\'85\u8221\'94 her eyes narrowed, \u8220\'93\u8230\'85have you been smoking?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Eddie straightened awkwardly, rubbing the back of his neck. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Uh, no. Not at all. Totally smoke-free lungs here. And\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 uh\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 sorry, Mom. But, how did you even get in? I locked my door this morning.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mrs. Welton did not answer. Instead, she reached into her coat pocket and drew out a wand, gnarled and sea-worn, as if it had been carved from driftwood. She tapped it against her palm, and the silence deepened.\line \line \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh\u8230\'85 that. Really? You\u8217\'92ve got to stop using the unlocking charm on my door, Mom. You do realize that\u8217\'92s technically an offence under Article Six of the new Ministry legislation on magical misuse, right?\u8221\'94\line \line \u8220\'93I am your mother, Edward. And you\u8217\'92d be one very ungrateful child to drag me into court over a law some daft minister scrawled out after three too many bottles of whiskey.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Eddie raised his hands in mock defeat, his voice rising in exasperation.\line \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Now you\u8217\'92re just insulting the Ministry! And you know that\u8217\'92s\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 But she cut him off, her voice low, urgent, carrying a weight that stilled the air.\line \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I didn\u8217\'92t come here to bicker with you, Edward. This is about your sister.\u8221\'94}
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Alright,\u8221\'94 Eddie sighed, \u8220\'93What did Torrie do this time?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \line \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Liese Academy summoned me earlier this morning. Your sister was caught in a duel\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 with three other students.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \line \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Whoa,\u8221\'94 Eddie chuckled, \u8220\'93Three at once? That\u8217\'92s\u8230\'85 kinda impressive. Did she win?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \line \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93That is not what you should be concerned about, Edward.\u8221\'94 Her tone was clipped, bristling with disapproval. \u8220\'93Your father and I still have orders to fill at the Apothecary. Which means you will go to the Academy and bring your sister home.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \line \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why me? Can\u8217\'92t you just wait until you\u8217\'92re done brewing whatever batch is left? She\u8217\'92s the one who caused trouble, not me.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \line \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I can\u8217\'92t, Edward. Not this time. I need you to do this for me.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0 \line \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93\u8230\'85Alright. Fine. I\u8217\'92ll go.\u8221\'94}
